n not attacked.  \n  3. **N: b8-c6** – develops knight toward center, prepares e5/d5.  \n  4. **P: c7-c5** – flank attack in center, Sicilian-style.  \n  5. **P: e7-e6** – flexible, supports d5.  \n\nReason:  \nThe most classical and solid response to 1. d3 is to occupy the center with 1...d5. It fights for space, prevents e2-e4 from being comfortable for White, and allows Black to develop naturally with ...Nf6, ...e6 or ...c5, etc. It’s a flexible and strong move that doesn’t create weaknesses. Other moves like ...e5 are also fine, but ...d5 is more direct and takes the center immediately. Knights moves like ...Nc6 are okay but less challenging to White’s setup.  \n\nAnswer: P: d7-d5'}
