nal for the black king's bishop. The pawn on d5 is already advanced, so e5 creates a strong pawn duo. It might lead to open positions favorable for piece play. Nf6 is also good but e5 is more aggressive and immediately challenges White's center. Given that White has a knight on a3 (a somewhat awkward square), Black can seize the initiative.\n\nAnswer: P: e7-e5"}
