y forward one rank from c2 to b1 (since black pawn moves downward, c2→b1 is forward one rank). That's a promotion capture. The valid move list includes P: c2-b1-q, P: c2-b1-n, etc. So promoting to queen is best. This is clearly winning.  \n\n4. Other moves like N: e5-d3 attacks king d2 and maybe fork something? d3 is empty, attacks king d2 and other squares. But promoting pawn to queen is far superior.  \n\nCheck if any tactical issues: After Pc2xb1=Q, white can capture queen? White rook b1 is captured. White's possible recapture: king d2 cannot take because queen protected? Actually queen from b1 is protected by black knight c1? Not necessarily, but black knight c1 is nearby. Also queen from b1 attacks king d2 (check). White king must move. King moves: d2→c1 (captures black knight? c1 is black knight), d2→c2 (captures pawn? c2 is gone), d2→d1, d2→e1, d2→e2, d2→e3. d2→c1 captures knight but then queen recaptures? Queen b1 takes knight? Wait if king moves to c1, queen b1 is attacking c1? Actually queen on b1 attacks c1 (adjacent diagonal). So king moving to c1 still in check from queen, illegal. So king cannot go c1. d2→c2 is empty, but queen b1 attacks c2 (diagonally), so still check. d2→d1 is attacked by queen b1 (straight down file b to d? No, queen b1 attacks d1 diagonally b1-d4 diagonal includes d4, not d1). Actually b1-d4 diagonal: b1, c2, d3, e4... so d1 not on same diagonal. d1 might be safe. But white rook b1 was captured, so d1 possibly not attacked. Need to visualize: queen b1 attacks vertically b-file (none), horizontally rank1 (a1,c1,d1,e1,f1,g1,h1). So queen attacks c1 (black knight), d1, e1, f1, etc. So d1 is attacked horizontally. So king cannot go to d1. e1 is attacked horizontally. e2 is attacked? Queen b1 to e2 is diagonal b1-e4, so e2 not on that diagonal; but e2 is same rank? No e2 rank2, queen rank1, so not horizontal. But e2 might be attacked by black pieces? Possibly.  \n\nWe should calculate quickly: After Pc2xb1=Q+, white king must move out 