pawns). After R: e4-e2+, white king must move. Likely Ke1 (or Kf1, Kg2, etc). Then black can play R: e2-a2, capturing the pawn. White is left with only king, and black can easily checkmate with king and rook, or even promote pawns at leisure. This line is simple and avoids any chance of stalemate because we keep the white king moving. Also, it immediately wins material and simplifies.\n\nThus, the chosen move is R: e4-e2.\n\nAnswer: R: e4-e2"}
